The grant
What happened
On October 1, 2026, the Supreme Court granted review of whether the detention statute requires holding, during removal proceedings, people who, like Ricardo Aparecido Barbosa da Cunha, are in the United States and were not admitted. The October 1 order grants review and does not decide the case. The bond statute is the statute under which the government may release someone.
1
2
17
The detention statute uses two descriptions of the person, an applicant for admission and an alien seeking admission. It tells an immigration officer to detain that person for a removal proceeding when the officer decides the person seeking admission is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted.
16
Detention statute
·
2024 compilation
·
Inspection of applicants for admission
in the case of an alien who is an applicant for admission, if the examining immigration officer determines that an alien seeking admission is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted, the alien shall be detained
16
Government Publishing Office
The Second Circuit held that Barbosa da Cunha may be an applicant for admission and is not seeking admission, and held that the detention statute does not apply to someone in the United States after entering without inspection and admission who was not apprehended while entering the country or shortly thereafter. The Second Circuit affirmed the district court, which had required a bond hearing or release. A bond hearing is a hearing on release. Release in his case followed a finding that he was not a danger and not a flight risk. The hearing did not itself require release. He has never been arrested for or charged with a crime. The Second Circuit's April 28 opinion calls Barbosa da Cunha the petitioner.
5
Under the Department of Homeland Security's July 10 memorandum, an applicant for admission may not be released from custody except by parole. That memorandum says the only people eligible for release on bond are people already admitted.
